### Using Wrap Widget

The `Wrap` widget automatically arranges its children in rows and wraps them to the next line when there is no more space in the current row.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

void main() {
  runApp(MyApp());
}

class MyApp extends StatelessWidget {
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return MaterialApp(
      home: Scaffold(
        appBar: AppBar(
          title: Text('Wrap Example'),
        ),
        body: Padding(
          padding: const EdgeInsets.all(16.0),
          child: Wrap(
            spacing: 8.0, // space between widgets
            runSpacing: 8.0, // space between rows
            children: List.generate(20, (index) {
              return Container(
                width: 100,
                height: 100,
                color: Colors.blue[100 * (index % 9)],
                alignment: Alignment.center,
                child: Text('Item $index', style: TextStyle(fontSize: 24)),
              );
            }),
          ),
        ),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

In this example:
- The `Wrap` widget is used to display items.
- `spacing` and `runSpacing` control the spacing between items and rows, respectively.
- `List.generate` is used to create a list of `Container` widgets, each representing an item.
- The size of each `Container` (width and height) is fixed, but you can adjust it as needed.

### Using GridView Widget

The `GridView` widget is another way to display a grid of items. You can specify the cross-axis count (i.e., the number of items per row).

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

void main() {
  runApp(MyApp());
}

class MyApp extends StatelessWidget {
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return MaterialApp(
      home: Scaffold(
        appBar: AppBar(
          title: Text('GridView Example'),
        ),
        body: Padding(
          padding: const EdgeInsets.all(16.0),
          child: GridView.builder(
            itemCount: 20,
            gridDelegate: SliverGridDelegateWithFixedCrossAxisCount(
              crossAxisCount: 3, // number of items per row
              crossAxisSpacing: 8.0, // space between items in the same row
              mainAxisSpacing: 8.0, // space between rows
            ),
            itemBuilder: (context, index) {
              return Container(
                width: 100,
                height: 100,
                color: Colors.blue[100 * (index % 9)],
                alignment: Alignment.center,
                child: Text('Item $index', style: TextStyle(fontSize: 24)),
              );
            },
          ),
        ),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

In this example:
- The `GridView.builder` is used to generate a grid of items.
- `SliverGridDelegateWithFixedCrossAxisCount` is used to define the number of items per row (`crossAxisCount`), as well as the spacing between items and rows.
- Each `Container` represents an item, similar to the previous example.

Choose the method that best fits your needs. `Wrap` is simpler for automatic layout, while `GridView` provides more control over the grid structure.

